**Smart pointers in one paragraph.** They tie a resource's lifetime to an object (RAII). `unique_ptr` is sole, move-only ownership with no overhead. `shared_ptr` is shared ownership with reference counting. `weak_ptr` observes a shared object without owning it and breaks reference cycles.

**Design.** Keep the count in a separate heap *control block* shared by all owners, so copies agree on the count.

```cpp
template <typename T>
class SharedPtr {
  struct Control { std::atomic<long> count{1}; };
  T* ptr_ = nullptr;
  Control* ctrl_ = nullptr;
  void release() {
    if (ctrl_ && ctrl_->count.fetch_sub(1, std::memory_order_acq_rel) == 1) {
      delete ptr_;
      delete ctrl_;
    }
  }
public:
  SharedPtr() = default;
  explicit SharedPtr(T* p) : ptr_(p), ctrl_(p ? new Control : nullptr) {}
  SharedPtr(const SharedPtr& o) : ptr_(o.ptr_), ctrl_(o.ctrl_) {
    if (ctrl_) ctrl_->count.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
  }
  SharedPtr(SharedPtr&& o) noexcept : ptr_(o.ptr_), ctrl_(o.ctrl_) {
    o.ptr_ = nullptr; o.ctrl_ = nullptr;
  }
  SharedPtr& operator=(SharedPtr o) noexcept {   // copy-and-swap
    std::swap(ptr_, o.ptr_); std::swap(ctrl_, o.ctrl_);
    return *this;
  }
  ~SharedPtr() { release(); }
  T& operator*() const { return *ptr_; }
  T* operator->() const { return ptr_; }
  T* get() const { return ptr_; }
  long use_count() const { return ctrl_ ? ctrl_->count.load() : 0; }
};
```

**Why it is correct.**
- Increment can be relaxed (you already hold a reference); the decrement uses acquire-release so that the thread that deletes sees all writes made by other owners before they released.
- Passing by value in `operator=` handles self-assignment and gives the strong exception guarantee.
- If `new Control` throws, free `p` (the real one uses a try/catch or `unique_ptr` internally).

**Thread-safety scope.** Different `SharedPtr` objects that share ownership can be copied and destroyed from different threads safely. The *same* `SharedPtr` object being modified from two threads, and the pointee `T`, are **not** protected; you need your own synchronisation. The standard library has the same guarantees.

**Extensions to mention.** Custom deleters and `make_shared` (one allocation for object plus control block), `weak_ptr` with a separate weak count, `enable_shared_from_this`, and cycles causing leaks.
